WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.040
Hey, welcome to Models &amp; Agents episode 91 on June 24th, 2026. Let's see what shipped,

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:09.540
plenty of model releases and agent benchmarks moving today.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.040
Claude now joins teams as a persistent org-wide Slack entity.

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:19.580
The third major shift in LLM interaction after websites and desktop apps.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:26.760
Deflash delivers up to 15 times throughput on Navidia Blackwell by drafting whole token blocks in

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.720
parallel instead of auto-regressive token by token generation.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:37.820
Quen released two new agent world MoE models trained to simulate tool, terminal, and GUI

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:44.820
environments rather than chat directly. OpenAI opened applications for DevDay 2026 in San Francisco

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:51.840
on September 29, with the keynote live streamed. The top story today centers on a new inference technique

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.580
from UC San Diego that changes how speculative decoding works at scale.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:02.620
Deflash replaces standard speculative decoding with a lightweight block diffusion model that drafts

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:10.600
entire token blocks in one forward pass. It conditions on target model hidden features via KV injection

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:18.920
and ships with 20 checkpoints plus support for SG Lang, VLLM, and tensor RT-LLM.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:27.220
The approach reports up to 6.08 times lossless speed up on Quen3, 8B, and NVIDIA measured

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:31.940
peaks of 15 times throughput on Blackwell hardware at fixed interactivity.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:37.180
Builders working on high throughput inference pipelines can now test the integration without

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:43.340
custom drafting logic. This update moves forward the ongoing conversation around AI, compute,

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:48.960
and inference economics that we tracked yesterday by showing measurable gains on existing serving

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:55.720
frameworks rather than new hardware requirements. Watch for community benchmarks on other model families

00:01:55.720 --> 00:02:00.920
and whether the block diffusion drafting generalizes beyond the reported setups.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:09.900
Quen released the agent world 35BA3B model, a 35 billion parameter mixture of experts with only

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:16.120
about 3 billion active parameters per token. The model was trained to predict environment responses

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:24.640
after agent actions across MCP, Terminal, SWE, Android, Web, and OS GUI domains.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:30.840
It functions as a language world model for simulating the observation side of agent loops

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:36.860
rather than a chat model. The release includes a hugging face checkpoint and is positioned for

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:43.260
offline evaluation, synthetic trajectory generation, and sandbox testing. Builders can use it to

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:48.680
generate training data or validate tool use workflows without repeatedly calling real environments.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:55.120
This release continues the open-weight models track we discussed yesterday by narrowing the gap

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.060
and specialized agent simulation rather than general chat performance.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:08.520
Baidu open sourced unlimited OCR, a 3.3 billion multilingual model under MIT license that performs one

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:16.340
shot full document parsing on single images, multi-page documents, and PDFs. It supports 32K output length

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:22.000
and offers base and Gundam image modes for different layouts, with inference available via

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:29.420
transformers or SG-lang, with open AI compatible streaming. The model targets deeper document

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:36.900
understanding beyond cropped region OCR. Teams handling long PDFs or mixed language archives should

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:44.820
tested this week against current pipelines. Quinn also released the larger agent world 397 BA-17

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:51.760
B sibling model on hugging face and the Quinn blog. It maintains the same environment simulation training

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:57.600
objective across agent interaction domains. Early discussion centers on its potential for higher

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:03.660
fidelity trajectory synthesis at scale. No detailed benchmarks were released with the announcement.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:11.980
Expo SQL adds clause level rewards derived from execution results and error messages to reinforcement

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:19.460
learning for text to SQL, replacing uniform query level rewards. It shows consistent gains over

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:26.040
supervised fine tuning, prompting, and prior RL baselines on standard benchmarks. Code is available

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:34.100
at the linked GitHub repository. Andre Carpathy described Claude Tag as the third major LLM UI-UX

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:40.680
paradigm. A self-contained persistent asynchronous entity with org-wide tools, memory, and context

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:47.740
that integrates directly into Slack. Once engineering work for security, compute, and integrations is

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:53.840
complete, teams can interact with it conversationally across workloads. The approach moves beyond

00:04:53.840 --> 00:05:00.800
website or desktop app models toward inline team participation. This development advances the agents

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:06.800
and tool use arc we covered yesterday by moving from experimental prototypes toward production team

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:13.420
integration patterns. Carpathy also highlighted an enterprise grade coding system that writes the

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:21.060
majority of code in team settings, deeply integrated and multiplayer inside Slack. It differs from simple

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:27.980
RGAS over Slack or OpenClaw style tools by functioning as a shared workspace where participants act

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:34.820
more like managers. The product has moved past hackathon stage through internal iteration and now

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:42.200
supports real deployments. Circle published a USDC specification for a machine payments protocol aimed

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:48.800
at AI agent transactions. The spec targets automated machine to machine payments without human

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:56.080
intermediaries. Early details focus on protocol design rather than live implementations. Applications

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:03.140
are now open for the September 29 open AI Dev Day event in San Francisco. With the opening keynote

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:09.860
available via live stream for remote attendees, the deadline is July 10. Developers planning to attend

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:19.340
or watch should apply soon. Community member on our slash local LLMA is testing a Psi E 5.0 16 times

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:26.480
slot split into two slash eight configuration via riser cable. The setup aims to accommodate both an

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:37.440
RTX 5070 Ti and an RTX 4070 within a single system. Daytime use focuses on agent workloads while evenings

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:43.740
shift to gaming activities. Token generation speeds stay acceptable when operating at 16,000

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:51.640
context length. Performance degrades noticeably when scaling context to 128,000 tokens under the split

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:57.660
configuration. The discussion centers on potential effects to the primary GPU from the bandwidth

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:03.940
division. This hardware experiment reflects practical considerations for multi GPU agent and

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:11.480
inference setups. The Mod-G-G-C-N paper from Ericsive introduces a modularity aware graph neural

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:18.360
network designed specifically for text classification tasks. It incorporates a modularity based auxiliary

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:24.400
loss function that encourages the formation of class coherent document communities within the

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:31.220
graph structure. The method decouples the original text G-C-N graph construction to achieve training

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:37.460
speed ups ranging from two to ten times. Performance improvements are particularly noted on data sets

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:43.780
exhibiting low homophiliy characteristics. This auxiliary loss helps the model better capture community

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:50.060
structures aligned with classification labels. Researchers can access the full details in the R-Craft

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:55.980
Preprint for implementation specifics. The approach target scenarios where standard graph neural

00:07:55.980 --> 00:08:02.060
networks struggle with disconnected class representations. Okay, let's pop the hood on clause

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:08.360
level rewards in reinforcement learning for text to SQL. Everyone talks about reinforcement learning

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:15.960
for text to SQL as simply add execution feedback and improve. In practice, the reward design itself

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:21.480
determines whether the model learns to fix individual clauses or just guesses whole queries.

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:29.920
Standard methods assign a single scalar reward to the entire generated SQL. So a query with one wrong

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:36.720
wear clause and correct select join still receives the same penalty as a completely broken query.

00:08:37.420 --> 00:08:46.240
Expo underscore aql instead parses execution errors and runs incremental clause execution to tag exactly

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:51.980
which clauses failed. This produces per clause learning signals that let the policy gradient update

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:58.640
only the faulty parts while reinforcing correct ones. The engineering cost is extra execution traces

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:05.540
per training example, but the paper shows this finer granularity outperforms both SFT and prior

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:12.080
RRL baselines on standard benchmarks. The approach works best when execution feedback is cheap and

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:18.740
deterministic. In domains where queries are expensive or non deterministic, the clause level signal

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:24.960
becomes noisy. Teams should prefer it when they already have reliable database sandboxes and want to

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:33.960
squeeze more signal from each failed generation. Test D-flash integration in VLLM or tensor RT-LLM

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:40.860
on a blackwell instance if you run high volume inference and want to measure the reported 6 to 15 times

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:48.960
throughput gains. Download the Quinn-Obench Quasiska even world 35ba3b checkpoint and use it to

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:54.240
generate synthetic trajectories for your own agent training loop instead of calling live tools every

00:09:54.240 --> 00:10:01.980
time. Apply for OpenAI DevDay 2026 before the July 10 deadline if you want to attend or watch the

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:09.980
keynote on new developer tooling. Try Unlimited-OCR on a multi-page mixed language PDF to compare full

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:16.080
document parsing speed and accuracy against your current pipeline. Keep an eye on the OpenAI DevDay

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:23.140
2026 keynote on September 29 with remote live stream option. Continued releases from the Quinn

00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:29.280
agent world family are expected as more environment simulation checkpoints appear. Further community

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:35.280
testing of D-flash across additional model families and serving frameworks is also on the way.

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:41.220
Tomorrow keep an eye on additional Quinn agent world checkpoints and early community numbers for

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:47.320
D-flash on non-blackwell hardware. That's models and agents for today. If you found this useful,

